    "updated_at": "2021-03-31T16:38:09Z",
    "body": "I think there's something going on here beyond just the configuration, because I tried what @rEnr3n described in an Arch VM pulled from Docker, and starship seemed to work correctly for me. I wonder if there's some compile-time flag for zsh which changes the default value of some options when not specified in the config or something (I've seen similar things in bash).\r\n\r\nHere's a `Dockerfile` that I'm using to generate the test:\r\n\r\n```\r\nFROM archlinux:latest\r\n\r\nRUN pacman -Syu --noconfirm rustup zsh base-devel\r\nRUN rustup install stable\r\n\r\nRUN cargo install starship\r\nRUN mv /root/.cargo/bin/starship /root/starship-cargo\r\n\r\nRUN curl -LOk https://github.com/starship/starship/releases/download/v0.51.0/starship-x86_64-unknown-linux-musl.tar.gz\r\nRUN tar -xf starship-x86_64-unknown-linux-musl.tar.gz\r\nRUN mv starship /root/starship-github\r\n\r\nRUN echo -e \"autoload -Uz promptinit\\npromptinit\\nprompt off\\neval $(/root/starship-github init zsh)\" >> /root/.zshrc\r\nENTRYPOINT zsh\r\n```\r\n\r\nIf I run `docker build . -t starshiptest` followed by `docker run -it starshiptest`, the prompt appears to work for me. @rEnr3n can you see if this works for you?",
