    "body": "@besselfunct Kind of.\r\n\r\nZSH is a complex beast because it comes with a whole ecosystem of \"plugins\" that can be loaded or unloaded, altering the behavior of ZSH. These are very useful, but sometimes can hide or change the functionality of the shell.\r\n\r\nHere's an experiment you can try: open a terminal and run `zsh -f`. This will cause ZSH to start without reading *any* configuration files. When I do this on a Pop_OS! 20.10 virtualbox, the `prompt` command no longer works: if I try to run `prompt off`, I get \"zsh: command not found: prompt\".\r\n\r\nIn order to enable the prompt system, you will need to run `autoload -U promptinit && promptinit`, which will then allow you to run the `prompt` command. The probable reason you can do this without needing to run the command usually is that your distro realized that almost everyone wants to do this anyways, so they added a line doing something like this in `/etc/zshrc`. This enables the prompt plugins so that you don't have to run these lines yourself, but as you've discovered, it can also be a little confusing and hide unintentional behavior.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
